    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Mozambicans carry a Portuguese first name (João, Armando, Graça, Felizarda) or a modern one (Edson, Dércio, Neusa, Cátia), followed by a family surname from their community. In the south, around Maputo, Gaza and Inhambane, surnames are Changana, Ronga and Chopi (Cossa, Macamo, Mabunda, Sitoe, Tembe, Nhantumbo). In the centre, around Beira and Tete, they are Sena, Ndau and Nyungwe (Simango, Sithole, Mandava, Phiri). In the north, Makhuwa families on the coast are largely Muslim and a father's first name often becomes the child's surname (Momade, Assane, Issufo). Some southern families also give Changana names with meaning, such as Xiluva (flower). Emakhuwa, Sena, Ndau, Chopi and Ronga are big languages in their own regions; in Maputo, Portuguese and Changana carry the day.",
